\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Pairing isometries transport incidence operators}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring. Give #{M,N,M',N'}
\vocabk{chosen perfect symmetric pairings}{fdip-001O}, and let
#{d:M\to N}, #{d':M'\to N'} be ordinary linear maps.
Suppose the linear isomorphisms #{P:M\to M'}, #{J:N\to N'}
preserve these pairings and satisfy #{d'P=Jd}.
Let #{W:N\to N}, #{W':N'\to N'} be self-adjoint maps with
#{W'J=JW}. For a fixed #{\mu\in k}, put
##{L=d^\dagger Wd,\qquad Q=L+\mu I_M,}
and define #{L',Q'} by the primed data. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
(d')^\dagger&=Pd^\dagger J^{-1}:N'\to M',\\
L'&=PLP^{-1},\qquad Q'=PQP^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
If #{Q} is an isomorphism, so is #{Q'}, and its
[inverse response](fdip-001Q) satisfies
##{G'=(Q')^{-1}=PQ^{-1}P^{-1}=PGP^{-1}.}
Consequently #{Qf=j} implies #{Q'(Pf)=Pj}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{z'\in M'}, pairing preservation gives
#{b_{M'}(Px,z')=b_M(x,P^{-1}z')}.
The [uniqueness of the adjoint](fdip-001P) therefore gives
#{P^\dagger=P^{-1}}, and similarly #{J^\dagger=J^{-1}}.
From #{d'=JdP^{-1}}, the adjoint product law gives
#{(d')^\dagger=Pd^\dagger J^{-1}}. Thus
##{\begin{aligned}
L'&=Pd^\dagger J^{-1}W'JdP^{-1}\\
  &=Pd^\dagger WdP^{-1}=PLP^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{J^{-1}W'J:N\to N} equals #{W}; every composition is typed.
The same scalar #{\mu} gives the assertion for #{Q}.
Multiplication in either order proves that #{PQ^{-1}P^{-1}} is the
inverse of #{Q'}. Finally #{Q'Pf=PQf=Pj}.}
}

\p{For [signed endpoint arrows](fdip-003B), the finite function
modules have [perfect counting duality](fdip-001F), including empty
carriers. Reindexing gives
##{\begin{aligned}
b_{M'}(Pf,Pg)&=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)g(v),\\
b_{N'}(Ja,Jb)&=\sum_{e\in E}\sigma(\varepsilon(e))^2a(e)b(e)
              =b_N(a,b).
\end{aligned}}
Multiplication by transported weights obeys #{W'J=JW}, by evaluation
at #{q(e)}. Thus the theorem applies without inverting any weight.
It also applies to the weaker signed-incidence symmetries. The weighted
edge pairing is #{b_W(a,b)=b_N(a,Wb)}; its adjoint of #{d} into the
counting vertex pairing is #{d^\dagger W}, whereas #{d^\dagger} uses
the counting edge pairing. The expression #{d^\dagger Wd} does not
require #{b_W} to be perfect.}

\p{The inverse hypothesis cannot be replaced by a unit mass scalar.
Over #{\mathbb Z}, a unit-weight edge on two vertices with #{\mu=1}
has #{Q=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}}.
Solving #{Q(x,y)=(1,0)} would require #{x=2y} and #{3y=1}, which
is impossible in #{\mathbb Z}. This #{Q} is not even surjective.}

\p{For automorphisms the conjugacies are commutation identities.
They concern symmetric bilinear pairings over fixed coefficients.
A grading is preserved only if #{P} preserves its graded pieces;
a star is preserved only if #{P} commutes with that star. A nontrivial
coefficient involution and Hermitian adjoint need their own conventions.}

\p{The proof uses the chosen duality construction in \ref{fdip-001P},
with the Hom convention of
\citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581}{stacks2017stacks}.
For real graph incidence, the energy convention is
\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}; the displayed
ring transport is a direct deduction, not a positivity theorem.
Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.reindexLinearEquiv} and
\leanref{Matrix.reindexRingEquiv} support its coordinate reindexing
specialization, without supplying the endpoint or pairing hypotheses.}
